"b8dff0b2-a40e-5488-9228-977e8100365b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479042 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186478562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479042 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479042 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479042 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186478562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479042 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d84637eb-2310-5af2-a7e5-b3d8297c7f31" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479053 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186478847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479053 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479053 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479053 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186478847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479053 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f1b8081d-9962-5728-8702-fe7f036192b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479075 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479075 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110287" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479075 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479075 #edb/ref 1071 "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(3)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $5$ |\n| $-2$ | $3$ | $-6$ | $-1$ | $-4$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479075 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "30cea08b-c33e-58ec-a947-197b4401903e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479075 #edb/ref 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function ${f}^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(x)=\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}+{g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(3)=\\frac{g(3)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}+{g}^{′}(3){f}^{-1}(3).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(3)=-2,$ $g(3)=-2,$ ${g}^{′}(3)=5,$ and ${f}^{′}(-2)=-6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(3) & =\\frac{g(3)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}+{g}^{′}(3){f}^{-1}(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{{f}^{′}(-2)}+5\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{-6}-10 \\\\  & =-\\frac{29}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479082 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479082 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342576" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479082 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479082 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9b/9bb238610c8f58efb4e0d1c41de0cec2fe2406e0f29e465237560b73cdb3618f.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(0,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(0,0)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479082 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e911702-eb82-5b97-88c2-1d3bfbe6d604" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479082 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $(0,0)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0)=0.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [-1,1]$ is $3$, hence, ${f}^{′}(0)=3.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479089 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479089 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342595" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479089 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479089 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(-1)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $1$ |\n| $5$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $-2$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479089 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "518b39e3-bc07-5491-9032-d91dac622690" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479089 #edb/ref 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)={g}^{′}(-1){f}^{-1}(-1)+\\frac{g(-1)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(-1)=5,$ $g(-1)=-2,$ ${g}^{′}(-1)=1,$ and ${f}^{′}(5)=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(-1) & ={g}^{′}(-1){f}^{-1}(-1)+\\frac{g(-1)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 5+\\frac{(-2)}{{f}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =5+\\frac{(-2)}{1} \\\\  & =5+(-2) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479102 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479102 #edb/ref 1069 "q-15649" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479102 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479102 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{5}{8}\\sqrt{x}$ and the point $P(4,\\frac{5}{4})$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(\\frac{5}{4},4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479102 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "854b6ede-01ab-595a-a1e4-df6af7f40d5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479102 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(4,\\frac{5}{4}).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{5}{8}\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{5}{16\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{5}{32}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(4,\\frac{5}{4})$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(\\frac{5}{4},4)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(\\frac{5}{4}) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(\\frac{5}{4}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{5}{32})} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{5}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479119 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479119 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342577" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479119 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479119 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/37/37067c32385be4b652765062bbaf4e413ec87cec2ec6d0d01c6cd5dce64d7d9a.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(0,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479119 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b47ad43a-e012-58c2-b33a-c151f7cd3d62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479119 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $(0,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0)=1.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [-1,3]$ is $\\frac{3}{2}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479132 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479132 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110128" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479132 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479132 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/89/89dee9fffb22501189ac9f972dc4bcf7e2ed3602ef000b8ef01f04ddccd2e2eb.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(3,3)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(3,3)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479132 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d295fd4d-8093-5953-9c94-5f7f1a143168" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479132 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $(3,3)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(3)=3.$\nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,6]$ is $\\frac{1}{3}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
